---
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';

const isDev = import.meta.env.DEV;
const cssUrl = isDev ? '/styles/global.css' : 'https://static.kdzu.org/styles/global.css';
const digSiteSrc = isDev
  ? '/images/wavefarm/dig-site.jpg'
  : 'https://static.kdzu.org/images/wavefarm/dig-site.jpg';
---

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Unearthing the New Afterlife Array - KDZU</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href={cssUrl} />
  </head>
  <body class="wavefarm-transmission-page">
    <Header />
    <main>
      <div class="main-content">
        <h1>Unearthing the New Afterlife Array</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">
          KDZU Recovers a Subterranean RF Network and the Caretakers Who Keep It Alive.
        </p>
        <p class="subtitle-secondary">KDZU’s Wave Farm 2026 Transmission Residency Submission</p>

        <section class="transmission-body">
          <p>
            KDZU’s Goldfield, Nevada, station location recently received an unsigned letter<a
              href="#transmission-fn-1"
              id="transmission-ref-1"
              class="transmission-footnote-ref"
              aria-describedby="transmission-fn-1">*</a> in the mail, postmarked but otherwise unadorned, containing a
            hand-drawn map, technical sketches, and a claim that the Memphis, Tennessee-based OMSA<a
              href="#transmission-fn-2"
              id="transmission-ref-2"
              class="transmission-footnote-ref"
              aria-describedby="transmission-fn-2">**</a> (Orange Mound Space Agency) robotics and radio transmission
            collective may have persisted beyond the 1994 “Great Raid” of the Memphis Pyramid.
          </p>

          <p>
            According to this letter, while many OMSA members scattered westward, the OMSA (Our Methods Seem Abstract)
            RF research group traveled northeast, establishing a provisional site in the northern Catskills near
            present-day Acra, New York. The letter describes this region as strategically chosen for its seclusion, low
            population density, and hilly terrain. These are conditions the residents of Wave Farm already understand to
            be favorable for experimental long-range transmission, signal occlusion, and off-grid communications.
          </p>

          <p>
            KDZU approaches the claims in this letter as an activation point for its tactical mythmaking activities: a
            prompt to investigate, reconstruct, and extend a speculative lineage of OMSA’s (One More Sexy Anarchist)
            anarchist transmission practices at the Wave Farm site. In response, KDZU proposes developing the New
            Afterlife Array, a Meshtastic-based (LoRa and LoRaWAN), long-range, off-grid communication network
            deployed across the Wave Farm site. KDZU will discover four nodes of the network, G, F, N, H, each a letter
            in the word GOLDFINCH, a common bird found in the Acra, NY area known for its “waveform”-style flight
            pattern. This will imply that 5 other nodes have yet to be unearthed.
          </p>

          <figure class="transmission-figure">
            <img
              src={digSiteSrc}
              alt="Black-and-white technical sketch of a rectangular excavation pit in open terrain, with a small box-like node and antenna at the bottom, a survey rod along the wall, and a mound of excavated soil nearby."
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <figcaption>Sketch of what a "dig site" and node might look like.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h2 class="transmission-h2">Each of these four nodes consists of five components</h2>
          <ul class="transmission-component-list">
            <li>
              <strong>Meshtastic radio:</strong> Handles LoRa mesh communication between nodes.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Interpreter brain:</strong> A Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W, connected to the Meshtastic node over
              USB/serial, running KDZU-authored code that interprets FRMPayload (Frame Payload) data into MIDI.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Sound Interpolator:</strong> An Akai MPC Sample workstation connected via USB MIDI that translates
              the signals into sound.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Sound Amplifier:</strong> A small self-powered speaker
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Power System:</strong> A small solar panel and charging system for the electronics.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            This system treats transmission as a durational, intergenerational process. Signals that move slowly,
            degrade, repeat, and require multiple human encounters to be fully perceived. Using LoRa mesh devices
            distributed throughout the landscape, the project will “revive” a once-maintained but now-failed
            very-slow-transmission network in which messages propagate across terrain, time, and participation, forming
            an evolving archive of interrupted transmissions, delays, and relays. Participants will also encounter
            fragments of OMSA’s (One Many Sometimes Always) surreptitious caregiving of the system: caregiver maintenance
            logs, instructions for future care, and transmission logbooks, translated into sound and text, to create a
            spatial experience. The network is an installed radio-enabled sonic instrument performing its artifacts. It is
            a contemporary anarchist instantiation of what J.C.R. Licklider once imagined as an “Intergalactic Computer
            Network,” reinterpreted at Wave Farm as a recently unearthed low-power, collectively maintained system
            contingent on human presence, proximity to others, and human+transmitter+receiver collaboration.
          </p>

          <p>
            At Wave Farm, the New Afterlife Array operates as both a field test of a long-dormant system and a proving
            ground for transmission infrastructures that resist centralization—privileging slowness over speed, and
            communication as an ongoing, collectivity-dependent negotiation.
          </p>

          <p>
            This work draws from the legacy of upstate New York’s many historical intentional community experiments I
            encountered while living in Troy, New York: utopias that collapsed, dispersed, or dissolved, yet left behind
            important traces, structures, and ideologies that continue to resonate in ways that can be more potent than
            the utopianists' original intentions and methods. The New Afterlife Array treats failures as symbolically
            generative transmissions that persist beyond their original intentions.
          </p>

          <h2 class="transmission-h2">FRMPayload (Frame Payload) Communications Messaging Signals</h2>
          <div class="transmission-table-wrap">
            <table class="transmission-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Signal</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>PLAY BELL</td></tr>
                <tr><td>SCENE GRAVE</td></tr>
                <tr><td>NODE 4 WAKE</td></tr>
                <tr><td>ALL HUSH</td></tr>
                <tr><td>ECHO 3</td></tr>
                <tr><td>TEMPO 62</td></tr>
                <tr><td>MUTE NORTH</td></tr>
                <tr><td>PANIC</td></tr>
                <tr><td>RELAY DUST</td></tr>
                <tr><td>CRYPT OPEN</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>Then each node has its own local mapping table, for example:</p>

          <h3 class="transmission-h3">NODE “G”</h3>
          <div class="transmission-table-wrap">
            <table class="transmission-table transmission-table--mapping">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Signal</th>
                  <th scope="col">Mapping</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>PLAY BELL</td>
                  <td>MIDI Note 36</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>SCENE GRAVE</td>
                  <td>Program Change 12</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>ALL HUSH</td>
                  <td>CC 7 to volume 20</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>TEMPO 62</td>
                  <td>internal sequencer rate change</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>ECHO 3</td>
                  <td>trigger sample 3 with long decay</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h3 class="transmission-h3">Additional distributed instrument components</h3>
          <ul class="transmission-meta-list">
            <li>
              <strong>sender-based behaviors:</strong> Node “G” responds to node “F” differently than node “N” or “H”
            </li>
            <li><strong>signal-strength mappings:</strong></li>
            <li><strong>packet delay memory:</strong></li>
            <li><strong>node-specific identities:</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="transmission-footnotes" aria-labelledby="transmission-notes-heading">
          <h2 id="transmission-notes-heading" class="transmission-footnotes-title">Notes</h2>

          <div id="transmission-fn-1" class="transmission-footnote-item" tabindex="-1">
            <p>
              <a href="#transmission-ref-1" class="transmission-footnote-back" aria-label="Back to reference">*</a>
              The unsigned letter referenced in this proposal draws inspiration from Letters to the Mount Wilson
              Observatory (1915–1935), a collection of 33 letters sent by members of the public to astronomers in
              California, often expressing highly idiosyncratic understandings of the universe. The letters were later
              compiled into the book <em>No One May Ever Have the Same Knowledge Again</em> and are exhibited at the
              Museum of Jurassic Technology in Los Angeles.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div id="transmission-fn-2" class="transmission-footnote-item" tabindex="-1">
            <p>
              <a href="#transmission-ref-2" class="transmission-footnote-back" aria-label="Back to reference">**</a>
              Members of OMSA (Ornery, Methodical, Sassy, and Angsty) developed their name as a deliberately unstable
              acronym, re-proclaimed with new words on each instantiation as a form of tactical opacity. This continual
              mutation of meaning functioned as a defensive gesture, preventing the group’s intentions, structure, or
              ideology from being fixed or fully knowable. This preserved the group’s capacity to operate as a
              distributed, evasive entity, untethered from specific affiliations, art genres, or creative frameworks.
              KDZU respectfully maintains this naming system, even though it seems the group has been inactive since
              1994.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </main>
    <Footer />
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  .wavefarm-transmission-page main {
    display: block !important;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr !important;
  }

  /* Stronger than global .subtitle: brighter, heavier, tuned for long dek */
  .wavefarm-transmission-page .subtitle {
    color: var(--color-bright-white);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 2.25rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
    max-width: 42rem;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .subtitle-secondary {
    display: inline-block;
    margin: 0 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #ff1493;
    line-height: 1.5;
    max-width: 42rem;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-body p {
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-h2 {
    font-size: 2rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-bright-white);
    margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
    max-width: 48rem;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-h3 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-bright-white);
    margin: 2rem 0 0.75rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
    max-width: 48rem;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-component-list,
  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-meta-list {
    list-style: disc;
    padding-left: 2rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    max-width: 48rem;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-component-list li,
  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-meta-list li {
    color: var(--color-light-text);
    margin-bottom: 0.65rem;
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-component-list strong,
  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-meta-list strong {
    color: var(--color-bright-white);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    max-width: 48rem;
    border-radius: 4px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-medium-gray);
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-table th,
  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-table td {
    padding: 0.65rem 1rem;
    text-align: left;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-medium-gray);
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-table th {
    background: var(--color-darker-gray);
    color: var(--color-bright-white);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-table td {
    color: var(--color-light-text);
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Monaco, Consolas, monospace;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-table tbody tr:last-child td {
    border-bottom: none;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-table--mapping td:first-child {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-figure {
    margin: 2rem 0;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-figure img {
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 4px;
    box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-figure figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--color-light-text);
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.4;
    max-width: 660px;
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-footnote-ref {
    color: #ff1493;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-decoration: none;
    vertical-align: super;
    font-size: 0.75em;
    margin-left: 0.05em;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-footnote-ref:hover,
  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-footnote-ref:focus-visible {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-footnotes {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-medium-gray);
    max-width: 48rem;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-footnotes-title {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-bright-white);
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-footnote-item {
    scroll-margin-top: 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-footnote-item:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-footnote-item p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--color-light-text);
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-footnote-back {
    color: #ff1493;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-decoration: none;
    margin-right: 0.35em;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-footnote-back:hover,
  .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-footnote-back:focus-visible {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  @media (max-width: 1440px) {
    .wavefarm-transmission-page .subtitle {
      font-size: 2rem;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 1024px) {
    .wavefarm-transmission-page .subtitle {
      font-size: 1.75rem;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .wavefarm-transmission-page .subtitle {
      font-size: 1.45rem;
      letter-spacing: 0;
    }

    .wavefarm-transmission-page .subtitle-secondary {
      font-size: 1rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    }

    .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-h2 {
      font-size: 1.65rem;
    }

    .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-h3 {
      font-size: 1.25rem;
    }

    .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-component-list li,
    .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-meta-list li {
      font-size: 1rem;
    }

    .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-figure {
      margin: 1.5rem 0;
    }

    .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-figure figcaption {
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      padding: 0 1rem;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .wavefarm-transmission-page .subtitle {
      font-size: 1.2rem;
    }

    .wavefarm-transmission-page .subtitle-secondary {
      font-size: 0.9375rem;
    }

    .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-h2 {
      font-size: 1.35rem;
    }

    .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-table th,
    .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-table td {
      padding: 0.5rem 0.65rem;
      font-size: 0.8125rem;
    }

    .wavefarm-transmission-page .transmission-table--mapping td:first-child {
      white-space: normal;
    }
  }
</style>
